# =====================================================================
#  citekey  ->  doi:10.xxxx/yyy   or   pmid:12345678
#
#  This is the ONLY bibliography file that is edited by hand, and it
#  contains identifiers only. Every field in paper/refs.bib is fetched
#  from the registrar by tools/fetch_refs.py. Nothing is typed from
#  memory. The comment above each entry records what the source
#  actually reports, in its own units, so that a claim can be checked
#  against it without opening the paper.
# =====================================================================

# --- the Saudi cohort, both anchors ---------------------------------
# 84 patients, 16 variants (7 PCCA / 9 PCCB), c.425G>A p.Gly142Asp in
# 59 families (70.2%), c.990dupT p.E331Xfs in 7 families (8.3%),
# all homozygous except ONE FAMILY (not one patient).
saudi84                 pmid:30705822

# --- enzymology -----------------------------------------------------
# alpha6beta6 dodecamer; alpha carries BC + BCCP, beta the carboxyltransferase.
pcc_structure           doi:10.1038/nature09302

# Human liver PCC purified: apparent Km for propionyl-CoA 0.29 mM.
pcc_liver_kinetics      pmid:6765947

# Four variant PCCs: NO significant difference in Km for propionyl-CoA
# vs wild type; ~half the kcat/Km; LESS THERMOSTABLE than wild type.
pcc_variants_kinetics   pmid:15890657

# --- event rates ----------------------------------------------------
# 0.5 PPY overall; 1.4 PPY at 3-6 y; 0.1 PPY at 13-17 y; 0.2 PPY adults.
mde_claims_us           doi:10.1186/s13023-025-03901-2

# 49/50 (98%) reported MDEs; mean annualized 1.0. GAMM/PAMM hazard on
# 3-HP + smooth time-since-last-MDE + participant random effect.
# 50% lower 3-HP -> RR 0.7; 50% lower C3 -> RR 0.6; 2-MC -> RR 1.0 (none).
natural_history         doi:10.1186/s13023-026-04251-3

# 269 patients, TriNetX electronic records 2015-2022. MDE = metabolic
# acidosis and/or hyperammonemia in an inpatient or ER setting.
# 0.53 PPY overall; U-shaped: 0.58 PPY at 0 to <2 y, 0.22-0.34 PPY at
# 2-18 y, 0.72 PPY in adults. 47.6% had at least one MDE in any setting.
mde_emr_us              doi:10.1016/j.ymgme.2025.109111

# --- crisis dynamics ------------------------------------------------
# Propionylcarnitine, propionylglycine, propionic acid essentially
# UNALTERED during acute decompensation. Free carnitine plasma, Table 2:
# no AMD 38.9 +/- 16.8 [7.0-93.3] n=56; AMD 22.0 +/- 12.4 [8.0-70.1] n=25;
# P = 0.0370. Discussion advises determining free carnitine during AMD to check
# whether supplementation is sufficient, and notes that a trend analysis on an
# individual patient basis could increase insight.
# Healthy ranges: C2 0.7-9.7 umol/L, C3 0.0-0.8 umol/L. NOTE: the C2/C3 ratio
# is a patients-versus-healthy comparison. Table 2 carries no C2, C3,
# acetylcarnitine or propionylcarnitine row tested against event status, so it
# supports no within-patient claim across a decompensation.
amd_phenotyping         doi:10.1186/s13023-020-1347-3

# Free carnitine 5.7 uM vs normal 37 +/- 8; short-chain acylcarnitines 28.6
# vs 5.7 +/- 3.5.
carnitine_pa_child      pmid:2771511

# Two PA and two MMA patients. Low plasma free carnitine, increased
# short-chain acylcarnitines. URINARY EXCRETION OF FREE CARNITINE DECREASED,
# while excretion of short-chain acylcarnitines, mostly propionylcarnitine,
# INCREASED. Total carnitine decreased in skeletal muscle. Proposed mechanism:
# mitochondrial propionylcarnitine, formed from free carnitine and excess
# propionyl-CoA, exchanges with free cytosolic carnitine and is then lost in
# the urine, causing secondary carnitine deficiency in the tissues.
# NOTE: the paper is Di Donato et al., not Roe et al.
carnitine_excretion_pa  doi:10.1016/0009-8981(84)90187-6

# Carnitine given to an MMA patient increased hippurate excretion (an index of
# intramitochondrial ATP and CoA availability), raised short-chain urinary
# acylcarnitines, and reduced methylmalonate and methylcitrate excretion.
# Carnitine acts by removing propionyl groups, RELEASING CoA and restoring ATP.
# The documented direction is therefore carnitine depletion -> CoA
# unavailability -> mitochondrial impairment, NOT the reverse.
coa_sequestration       doi:10.1136/adc.58.11.916

# Carnitine transporter solubilised from RAT renal apical (brush-border)
# membrane and reconstituted into liposomes; "corresponds, very probably, to
# the OCTN2 protein". First-order antiport, Na+-stimulated. "Substrate
# analogues inhibited the transport; the most effective were acylcarnitines
# and betaine." "Besides carnitine, only acylcarnitines and betaine were
# efficiently translocated." Km for carnitine 0.08 mM external, 1.2 mM
# internal. IN VITRO, RAT: the limitation is stated in the text.
octn2_renal_transport   doi:10.1016/j.bbamem.2003.12.001

# --- the reference standard for capacity ----------------------------
# Modified 1-13C-propionate breath test in 57 MMA subjects (19 transplant
# recipients) and 16 healthy volunteers. Isotopomer enrichment 13CO2/12CO2 in
# exhaled breath after an enteral bolus of sodium-1-13C-propionate, normalised
# for CO2 production. TEST REPEATABILITY: mean coefficient of variation 6.9%
# in controls and 12.8% in patients, "despite widely variable serum
# methylmalonic acid concentrations in the patients". Lower oxidation in
# mut0 and cblB; near normal in cblA and mut-; complete restoration to
# control levels after liver transplant. NOTE: the cohort is MMA, NOT PA.
breath_test_mma         doi:10.1038/s41436-021-01143-8

# --- the therapy ----------------------------------------------------
# 16 participants, 346 doses / 15.69 person-years. 70% risk reduction
# among the 8 who reported MDEs pretreatment. Single-arm.
trial_phase12           doi:10.1038/s41586-024-07266-7
pkpd_translational      doi:10.1089/nat.2022.0036

# 0.6 mg/kg biweekly selected from simulation in 1000 virtual
# participants aged 0-36 y using body-weight distributions, with 3-HP
# and 2-MC as the response. Conference abstract.
# NOTE: the task sheet listed this under refs.manual.bib on the
# assumption that it has no resolvable DOI. It does: doi.org content
# negotiation returns full CrossRef metadata for 10.70534/JVQU8233, so
# it belongs here, where the fields are fetched rather than typed.
dose_selection_poster   doi:10.70534/JVQU8233

# --- pathophysiology ------------------------------------------------
# Systematic analysis of the pathophysiology of each prevalent and rare
# complication of PA and MMA; mitochondrial impairment is the common
# hypothesis. Part 2 of the pair covers treatment and is not cited here.
pathophys_complications doi:10.1002/jimd.12129

# --- Saudi newborn screening: three cohorts, three incidences -------
# 40,965 newborns, Riyadh (King Fahad Medical City). PA 1:10,241. The
# authors note that false positives can inflate incidence estimates.
nbs_riyadh_kfmc         doi:10.3390/ijns10040072
# 775,000 newborns, 139 hospitals, national programme. PA 1:14,000 and
# the highest incidence among the inborn errors screened.
nbs_saudi_national      doi:10.1111/jpc.13469
# 56,632 infants, Prince Sultan Military Medical City, Riyadh.
# Propionic aciduria the most common metabolic disorder, 1:14,158.
nbs_psmmc               doi:10.15537/smj.2020.7.25147

